## What's actually in the file

```tsx
// src/Card.tsx
export function Card({ children }: { children: React.ReactNode }) {
  return (
    <div
      style={{ width: 800, height: 600, maxWidth: 1024, padding: 24,
               background: "#ff00aa", color: "#0a0a0a", borderColor: "#cccccc",
               boxShadow: "0 2px 4px rgb(0, 0, 0)", outlineColor: "#1a73e8" }}
      onClick={() => console.log("clicked")}
    >
      <div style={{ width: 720, fontSize: 18, color: "#333333" }}>{children}</div>
      <div style={{ width: 240, fontSize: 14, color: "#666666" }}>sidebar</div>
    </div>
  );
}
```

All three findings share one root cause: the component styles itself with an inline `style` object, which is exactly the escape hatch that bypasses the token scale (`tailwind.config.ts`), bypasses the responsive utility system, and lets a plain `<div>` masquerade as a control. So the three fixes are one refactor with three distinct payoffs.

`crimes context` adds two multipliers worth naming: **test gap is top-quartile** (no test file matched `Card` at all) and **blast radius is 0 importers**. Zero importers means this is a safe refactor to do aggressively — nothing downstream depends on the current markup. Top-quartile test gap means nothing will catch you if you get it wrong, so the changes below are deliberately structural rather than clever.

---

## Change 1 — `design_token_escape` (crime_00002, 9 raw values, confidence 0.75)

**Fix shape from the finding:** *replace raw values with the design token.*

The complication `crimes` can't see: **the tokens don't exist yet.** `tailwind.config.ts` defines exactly three colors — `brand #0050ff`, `surface #f7f7fa`, `ink #111111` — and none of the nine literals in `Card.tsx` match any of them. There is no spacing scale, no radius scale, no shadow scale. So "replace with the token" is really two steps: extend the scale, then consume it.

```ts
// tailwind.config.ts
export default {
  theme: {
    colors: {
      brand: "#0050ff",
      "brand-accent": "#1a73e8",  // was Card.tsx outlineColor
      surface: "#f7f7fa",
      "surface-raised": "#ff00aa", // ⚠️ needs design sign-off — see below
      ink: "#111111",
      "ink-muted": "#333333",      // was inner div color
      "ink-subtle": "#666666",     // was sidebar color
      border: "#cccccc",           // was borderColor
    },
    extend: {
      boxShadow: { card: "0 2px 4px rgb(0 0 0 / 0.2)" },
    },
  },
};
```

Two of these mappings are honest promotions of an existing value (`#1a73e8` → a focus accent, `#cccccc` → a border token). Three are new text/border ramp entries that a design system would plausibly want anyway. **`#ff00aa` is the one I would not auto-promote** — magenta against `ink #111111` is not a considered surface color, it reads like a placeholder someone never replaced. I've stubbed it as `surface-raised` so the refactor is mechanical, but flag it for a designer; the alternative is that the card should simply use the existing `surface` token and the magenta was always a bug.

This is the change that most directly buys down agent risk. The finding's own summary names the mechanism: *"agents extending the file may copy them forward without realising they bypass the tokens."* Once the values live in the config, the next agent that adds a row to this card copies `text-ink-subtle`, not `#666666`.

## Change 2 — `accessible_interaction_risk` (crime_00001, medium, confidence 0.85)

**Fix shape from the finding:** *use semantic elements; add ARIA only when semantics don't fit.*

The suggested action offers two paths — hoist to `<button>`, or add `role="button"` + `aria-label` + `tabIndex={0}`. **Take the first one, but not by wrapping the whole card.**

The signature is `{ children: React.ReactNode }`, so this card accepts arbitrary content — including links and buttons. Wrapping that in `<button>` produces invalid HTML (interactive content inside interactive content) and a screen-reader experience where the nested controls are unreachable. The `role="button"` path has the same problem *and* obliges you to hand-write Enter/Space key handling that a real `<button>` gives you free.

So: keep the container non-interactive and give the interaction its own semantic element.

```tsx
{onActivate && (
  <button
    type="button"
    onClick={onActivate}
    className="mt-4 rounded-md bg-brand px-4 py-2 text-surface
               focus-visible:outline-2 focus-visible:outline-offset-2
               focus-visible:outline-brand-accent"
  >
    {actionLabel}
  </button>
)}
```

Note this is where `#1a73e8` earns its keep — as a real focus ring, which is what an `outlineColor` on a non-focusable div was never going to deliver.

Also worth deleting outright: the current handler is `() => console.log("clicked")`. It has no behaviour to preserve. Promoting it to an `onActivate` prop is me guessing that a real one was intended; if the click was always vestigial, **remove it entirely** and the finding clears with less code, not more.

If a product requirement genuinely demands the entire card surface be clickable, the fallback is the ARIA path — but it is strictly worse and should carry a comment saying why it was chosen:

```tsx
<div
  role="button"
  tabIndex={0}
  aria-label={actionLabel}
  onClick={onActivate}
  onKeyDown={(e) => {
    if (e.key === "Enter" || e.key === " ") { e.preventDefault(); onActivate?.(); }
  }}
>
```

## Change 3 — `responsive_fragility` (crime_00003, 3 fixed values, confidence 0.65)

**Fix shape from the finding:** *drive layout from container queries or a fluid scale, not pixel media queries.*

The finding cites `width: 800`, `width: 720`, and `fontSize: 18` with `no @media in file`. Container queries are the right call here specifically because `Card` is a component with **0 importers today** — it has no idea what column it will eventually be dropped into, so sizing off the viewport would be wrong even if you added media queries.

- `width: 800` → `w-full max-w-5xl` (1024px ceiling, matching the existing `maxWidth`), so the card shrinks below 800px instead of forcing a horizontal scrollbar.
- `width: 720` / `width: 240` → flex children (`flex-1` + a fixed-basis sidebar) that stack vertically until the *container* is wide enough.
- `fontSize: 18` → `text-lg`. The real win is rem over px: an 18px literal ignores a user's browser font-size setting, which is an accessibility regression that this detector catches incidentally.

I'd also drop `height: 600`, which the detector did **not** flag but which fails the same way — fixed height plus variable `children` equals clipped or overflowing content. Letting content determine height removes the problem rather than managing it.

