## Proposed `src/Card.tsx`

```tsx
type CardProps = {
  children: React.ReactNode;
  /** Optional card action. Omit for a purely presentational card. */
  onActivate?: () => void;
  actionLabel?: string;
};

export function Card({ children, onActivate, actionLabel = "Open card" }: CardProps) {
  return (
    <section
      className="@container w-full max-w-5xl rounded-lg border border-border
                 bg-surface-raised p-6 text-ink shadow-card"
    >
      <div className="flex flex-col gap-6 @2xl:flex-row">
        <div className="min-w-0 flex-1 text-lg text-ink-muted">{children}</div>
        <aside className="w-full text-sm text-ink-subtle @2xl:w-60 @2xl:shrink-0">
          sidebar
        </aside>
      </div>

      {onActivate && (
        <button
          type="button"
          onClick={onActivate}
          className="mt-4 rounded-md bg-brand px-4 py-2 text-surface
                     focus-visible:outline-2 focus-visible:outline-offset-2
                     focus-visible:outline-brand-accent"
        >
          {actionLabel}
        </button>
      )}
    </section>
  );
}
```

Container-query variants (`@container`, `@2xl:`) are native in Tailwind v4; on v3 add `@tailwindcss/container-queries`. The fixture's `package.json` pins no Tailwind version, so confirm before assuming v4.

## A latent bug the refactor happens to fix

Worth calling out because no detector reported it: the current layout **already overflows**. The outer div is `800px` wide with `24px` padding on each side, leaving `752px` of content box — but the two children are `720px` and `240px`, totalling `960px`. The sidebar overflows its parent today, at every viewport, before responsiveness enters the picture. This is a good illustration of why `responsive_fragility` fires on fixed pixel widths in the first place: hard-coded sibling widths don't compose, and nobody notices until the numbers stop adding up. The flex layout in Change 3 makes the arithmetic impossible to get wrong.

## Verification

Since blast radius is 0 and test gap is top-quartile, the refactor is unverified by anything except the scanner. Re-running `crimes scan` on the fixture should report `total: 0` — all three findings are file-scoped with no cross-file dependencies. If you want real coverage, the cheapest high-value test is a keyboard one: render `Card`, tab to the action, press Enter, assert `onActivate` fired. That's the assertion that would have caught crime_00001 in the first place.

